      "math_academy_id": "q-3300",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function",
      "problem": "Find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$  as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x),$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{3}+2x+6.$",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=4{x}^{3}+2x+6,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=4{y}^{3}+2y+6\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{3}+2y+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =12{y}^{2}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{12{y}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
