    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-3302",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function",
      "problem": "If $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+9,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{x}^{2}+9,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{y}^{2}+9\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =4y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
