    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-51349",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function",
      "problem": "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}),$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}),$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{y})\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{y})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{y})\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{y})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & ={e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}) \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
