    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-73083",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function",
      "problem": "If $g(x)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =2\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{sec}⁡2y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
